### What is a Min Stack?

A Min Stack supports push, pop, top, and getMin, all in O(1) time. A plain stack would require scanning all elements to find the minimum, which is O(n). The trick is a second stack that tracks running minimums in lockstep with the main stack.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class MinStack {
    private final Deque<Integer> main = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<Integer> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void push(int val) {
        main.push(val);
        mins.push(mins.isEmpty() ? val : Math.min(val, mins.peek()));
    }

    public void pop() { main.pop(); mins.pop(); }
    public int top() { return main.peek(); }
    public int getMin() { return mins.peek(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinStack ms = new MinStack();
        ms.push(5); ms.push(3); ms.push(7); ms.push(2);
        System.out.println("Min: " + ms.getMin());
        ms.pop();
        System.out.println("After pop: " + ms.getMin());
    }
}
```

```text
Min: 2
After pop: 3
```

### Why does the two-stack approach work?

At each level the min stack entry records the smallest value among all elements from the bottom up to that point. Because the stacks are popped in lockstep, removing the top also removes its min record, and the new top is the correct minimum for whatever remains.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class MinStackInvariant {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<Integer> main = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Deque<Integer> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();
        int[] vals = {5, 3, 7, 2, 8};

        for (int v : vals) {
            main.push(v);
            mins.push(mins.isEmpty() ? v : Math.min(v, mins.peek()));
            System.out.println("Push " + v + ": min=" + mins.peek());
        }
        while (!main.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Pop " + main.pop() + ": min=" + mins.pop());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Push 5: min=5
Push 3: min=3
Push 7: min=3
Push 2: min=2
Push 8: min=2
Pop 8: min=2
Pop 2: min=2
Pop 7: min=3
Pop 3: min=3
Pop 5: min=5
```

### How do you implement it with BigDecimal for money?

Banking amounts use `BigDecimal`. Comparisons use `compareTo` rather than `<`. The logic is the same: push the smaller of the new value and the current min onto the auxiliary stack.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class MinStackMoney {
    private final Deque<BigDecimal> main = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<BigDecimal> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void push(BigDecimal val) {
        main.push(val);
        BigDecimal curMin = mins.isEmpty() ? val
                : (val.compareTo(mins.peek()) <= 0 ? val : mins.peek());
        mins.push(curMin);
    }

    public BigDecimal pop() { mins.pop(); return main.pop(); }
    public BigDecimal getMin() { return mins.peek(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinStackMoney ms = new MinStackMoney();
        ms.push(new BigDecimal("50000.00"));
        ms.push(new BigDecimal("30000.00"));
        ms.push(new BigDecimal("75000.00"));
        System.out.println("Lowest balance: " + ms.getMin());
    }
}
```

```text
Lowest balance: 30000.00
```

### What is the space-optimized variant?

Push to the min stack only when the new value is less than or equal to the current minimum. Pop from the min stack only when the popped value equals the current minimum. Use `<=` not `<` so duplicate minimums are tracked correctly.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class MinStackOptimized {
    private final Deque<Integer> main = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<Integer> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void push(int val) {
        main.push(val);
        if (mins.isEmpty() || val <= mins.peek()) mins.push(val);
    }

    public void pop() {
        if (main.pop().equals(mins.peek())) mins.pop();
    }

    public int getMin() { return mins.peek(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinStackOptimized ms = new MinStackOptimized();
        ms.push(3); ms.push(3); ms.push(5);
        ms.pop(); // pop 5
        System.out.println("Min: " + ms.getMin()); // 3
        ms.pop(); // pop 3 (duplicate)
        System.out.println("Min: " + ms.getMin()); // 3 (still correct)
    }
}
```

```text
Min: 3
Min: 3
```

### Why does <= matter for duplicates?

With strict `<`, pushing the same minimum twice only creates one min entry. Popping one copy removes that entry, leaving the other copy with no min record. Using `<=` creates an entry for each copy, so popping one still leaves the other correctly tracked.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DuplicateMinExplanation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("With <:  push 3,3 -> mins=[3]. Pop 3 -> mins=[]. Bug!");
        System.out.println("With <=: push 3,3 -> mins=[3,3]. Pop 3 -> mins=[3]. Correct.");
    }
}
```

```text
With <:  push 3,3 -> mins=[3]. Pop 3 -> mins=[]. Bug!
With <=: push 3,3 -> mins=[3,3]. Pop 3 -> mins=[3]. Correct.
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using strict `<` in the optimized variant is the subtlest bug. Forgetting to pop the min stack on pop is another. For the simple two-stack version, using `int` comparison on `Integer` objects beyond the cached -128..127 range can fail; using `equals()` or unboxing to `int` avoids this.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MinStackMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Mistake 1: strict < in optimized variant -> duplicate min bug");
        System.out.println("Mistake 2: forget to pop min stack on pop() -> drift");
        System.out.println("Mistake 3: == on Integer objects > 127 -> wrong comparison");
        System.out.println("Fix: use .equals() or unbox to int");
    }
}
```

```text
Mistake 1: strict < in optimized variant -> duplicate min bug
Mistake 2: forget to pop min stack on pop() -> drift
Mistake 3: == on Integer objects > 127 -> wrong comparison
Fix: use .equals() or unbox to int
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, a risk dashboard tracks a customer's intraday balance as positions open and close. The desk constantly needs the lowest balance to check overdraft thresholds. The min stack answers that in O(1), and reversing a position by popping immediately surfaces the correct previous minimum.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RiskDashboard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinStackMoney tracker = new MinStackMoney();
        tracker.push(new BigDecimal("100000.00"));
        tracker.push(new BigDecimal("85000.00"));
        tracker.push(new BigDecimal("120000.00"));
        tracker.push(new BigDecimal("70000.00"));

        System.out.println("Lowest intraday: " + tracker.getMin());
        tracker.pop(); // reverse last position
        System.out.println("After reversal: " + tracker.getMin());
    }
}
```

```text
Lowest intraday: 70000.00
After reversal: 85000.00
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Three approaches exist: two stacks in lockstep (O(n) extra, clearest), optimized push-only-on-new-min (less space, trickier pop), and delta encoding in a single stack (O(1) extra, hard to read). For interviews, present the clean two-stack version first and mention optimizations to show depth.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MinStackApproaches {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Two stacks in lockstep: O(n) space, clear");
        System.out.println("2. Push only on <=: less space, tricky pop");
        System.out.println("3. Delta encoding: O(1) extra, hard to read");
        System.out.println("Interview: present 1, mention 2 and 3");
    }
}
```

```text
1. Two stacks in lockstep: O(n) space, clear
2. Push only on <=: less space, tricky pop
3. Delta encoding: O(1) extra, hard to read
Interview: present 1, mention 2 and 3
```
